    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm looking a bit deeper into the intermediate stages, specifically the Amadori rearrangement. Can you explain the transition from the N-substituted glycosylamine to the Amadori products? I'm also trying to understand how the specific structure of different amino acids—like how lysine differs from others—influences the final aromatic profile of the cooked food.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Amadori rearrangement is a crucial isomerization pathway where the initial N-substituted glycosylamine undergoes a series of proton transfers and bond rearrangements to form a stable Amadori product. While many amino acids participate, lysine has a relatively reactive $\\epsilon$-amino group, which often enables it to undergo specific side-chain reactions. The structure of the amino acid dictates the fragmentation patterns during subsequent degradation, meaning different amino acids contribute distinct volatile compounds to the final aromatic profile of the cooked food.",
      "source": "target",
