      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under intense heat, Amadori products undergo dehydration, fragmentation, and polymerization to form melanoidins, which are large, brown, nitrogen-containing polymers responsible for the coloring. Fragmentation pathways yield smaller, highly reactive intermediates, such as dicarbonyls, which then react further to create diverse heterocyclic compounds. These resulting heterocyclic molecules, often containing nitrogen, are the primary contributors to the characteristic, complex, roasted and savory odors in cooked foods.",
      "source": "target",
